    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "That was my bill. That was what happened was Again, I won my election. I won the recount, but I was in court. So I went to the inaugural, and I had done a lot of USO tours. So I was friends with Paul Reichhoff, who's head of the AIVA, Iraq and Afghanistan, but it's America. So I went to their celebration, and I met a wounded vet in a wheelchair who had a service dog. and Louis Monthelban. And he said, I couldn't be here if it weren't for Tuesday. It was a dog. I said, well, tell me your story. And he was wounded and wrong. He came back, lived in Brooklyn, was isolated, never left his apartment, was drinking. Some organization contacted him, said, we want to pair you with a service dog. He goes to Connecticut, gets paired with Tuesday. And I said, what do they do for you? He said, well, he can sense the smell when I'm going to get a panic attack and nuzzle me and prevent me from having it. I sometimes have these nightmares, these debilitating nightmares, and he'll jump on the bed and wake me up. If I don't take my medications at the right time, he'll grab me by my sleeve and take me for the medicine. Said I was isolated, you have to take a dog out twice a day. And people don't like going up to scruffy looking wounded vets. They do like going up to scruffy looking wounded vets of a beautiful dog. And he went to grad school. became a writer, he wrote about it. So I had six months until I was seated, and the only thing I could do is raise money. I didn't talk about it. I was told don't shut up, just let it go. I just made it my business to find out about service dogs. As soon as I got to the Senate, I dropped a bill to do a study to see what the effect of having a service dog for a vet with PTSD. And it was a three-year study matching 200 vets with PTSD with their dogs. The VA fucked up the study over and over again. It took 10 years. It took 10 years. And I was gone by then. But finally, when it came out, the effects were enormous. The benefits are enormous. And that's when we got the pause act. And so now vets will be able, more and more vets will be able to get service dogs, 20 vets commit suicide every day. And this, there's nothing like a dog. There's nothing like a dog in a service. Service dogs are amazing. So when the study came out, I just cried.",
